  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 134 PM EST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nTwo jet streams are across the United States to start this \nperiod, with a Pacific Jet across the northern tier of the CONUS\nand a subtropical jet sliding eastward through the Gulf. After \na dry day today, another wave overtopping the ridging in the \nsouthwest dives southeastward into the Ohio River Valley early \nWednesday morning. It will attempt to tap into some moisture \nlocated across the Central Plains, but it appears that the warm \nsector will stay south of us or potentially get into our \nsouthern tier of counties (white to Jay County) with a couple \nhundred Joules of instability. SPC has the area in a general \nthunder outlook for Wednesday for the bulk of the area, but just\na small area of a marginal for wind. Its a small period \nWednesday afternoon/evening when severe components (mid level\nlapse rates and low level instability) have their best\npresentation. Otherwise, this area of low pressure is expected\nto supply some rain to the area starting late morning or midday\ninto the afternoon Wednesday. \n\nThen, as the main area of low pressure leaves the area, cold \nair wraps around its northwestern side and penetrates Lk MI \nThursday night into Friday. At MGC, bufkit RRFS soundings \nindicate saturation occurs around 0z with around 20 to 25C delta\nTs. As the cold air continues to filter south and eastward, \nbufkit RRFS soundings have snow beginning to mix in after 6z \nwith all snow not too long after 12z and there are still some \nmodel differences in their handling of ptype. All of this lake \neffect precip stays west of IN-15 and most of it stays west of \nUS-31. There will be some meandering of the band to some extent\nwith some of the precip passing east of US-31, but most of it \nstays west. Surface temperatures are still forecast to rise into\nthe low 40s as soon as Friday, which would indicate a change \nback to rain during the day Friday. However, the lake effect \ninstability is expected to continue through Saturday and it is \nvery possible that at least lake clouds continue for Sunday \ndownstream. Some 20 to 25 mph gusts will also be possible \nThursday within the gradient. \n\nTrajectories finally rotate around to southwesterly by Monday \nand temperatures trend back through the 40s this weekend to \nachieving 50s on Tuesday. So below normal temps final become\naround normal by then.",
